gainst the role requirements Step 3 - Interview If shortlisted, you’ll be invited to share your experience and learn more about the role. As part of the interview process, we may take references and complete qualification and/or background checks. Step 4 - Offer If successful, we’ll send you a formal offer and guide you through the onboarding process. Step 5 - Prepare to start A member of our team will be available to support you as you get ready to join Adelaide University. We understand moving to a new workplace can be a big change, so we've created some practical tips to help you navigate the transition smoothly. Tips for a successful transition to AU Need adjustments? We’re committed to ensuring our recruitment process is accessible to everyone. If you require adjustments at any stage, please let us know and we’ll work with you to make sure you’re supported. Make a request First Nations Employment Adelaide University aspires to be a university of choice for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Peoples and is committed to reconciliation. Our future strategies towards Indigenous advancement include a focus on elevated employment outcomes for First Nations Peoples, with the University’s new Enterprise Agreement including multiple targeted employment benefits and outcomes for First Nations Peoples. As a part of this commitment Adelaide University has a dedicated First Nations Employment Advisor whose role is to provide strategic direction, advise on processes and procedures, support existing staff in their professional development and as a touch point for First Nations people interested in employment opportunities at Adelaide University, Tirkangkaku. For a culturally safe and confidential conversation regarding First Nations Employment at Adelaide University, Tirkangkaku, please contact Alison Hughes, Senior First Nations Employment Advisor at first.nations.employment@adelaide.edu.au Our commitment to you We empower our people to bring their unique contributions to discover, engage and thrive in work that matters. We’re committed to creating a workplace where everyone can succeed. Here’s some of what you’ll find working at Adelaide University: Inclusive culture: We welcome people from all backgrounds and celebrate diversity of thought and experience. Flexibility: Life is dynamic, and we support flexible working to help you thrive. Growth and discovery: From professional development programs to research opportunities, your ambition has room to grow here. Benefits: Enjoy 17% superannuation and a comprehensive program of benefits. Pay and benefits Until a new Adelaide University Enterprise Agreement (EA) is established, employment conditions are guided by foundation university enterprise agreements. Anyone joining us prior to this will be employed under the University of Adelaide Enterprise Agreement or a Senior Staff contract. View University of Adelaide EA View University of South Australia EA Life at Adelaide University Adelaide University stands for progress. We are a university for the future, committed to making a transformative impact on a local and global scale. Our spirit of collective advancement drives future-defining research and innovative teaching that launches rewarding careers. Positive change is at the heart of everything we do. We champion bold ideas that lead to new firsts and significant shifts. Guided by curiosity, we challenge the status quo for a future that is better for all. Whoever you are, wherever you’re from, Adelaide University has the world-class disciplines, people and connections to launch your future. Meet our people At Adelaide University, our people are at the heart of everything we do. Read more about a typical day for people who work here. Thomas, Academic Tutor Meet Thomas Chelsea, Senior Lecturer Meet Chelsea Cameron, Senior Project Officer Meet Cameron Ben, Senior Project Officer Meet Ben Peta, Lecturer Meet Peta Kym, Manager, Marketing and Communications Meet Kym Staff video spotlight series Every role contributes to our shared mission. From researchers making global breakthroughs to educators creating outstanding student experiences, and professional staff driving innovation behind the scenes. Hear from the people shaping the future of our university and beyond. Meet Professor Tina Brodie: Pro Vice Chancellor First Nations Education Meet Paul Havelberg: Regional Director Whyalla Campus Meet Dr Sita Withers: Senior Lecturer Meet Professor Joanne Cys: Provost and Deputy Vice Chancellor Meet Professor Craig Batty: Pro Vice Chancellor – College of Creative Arts, Design and Humanities Meet Dr Chelsea Cutting: Senior Lecturer, School of Education Meet Mark Cherrill: Facilities Manager Meet Natasha Boots: Director of Student Wellbeing and Safety Meet Vince Condina: Chief Technology Officer Meet Associate Professor Beth Loveys: School of Agriculture, Food and Wine Meet Professor Andrew Zannettino: Pro Vice Chancellor – College of Health Meet Ally Miliado: Protocol Officer Living and working in South Australia South Australia offers the perfect blend of lifestyle and opportunity. With vibrant culture, stunning natural surroundings and a welcoming community, it’s a city that supports both personal and professional growth. Whether you're relocating or already here, discover why South Australia is a great place to call home. Life in Adelaide Explore what makes SA great – from unmatched natural beauty to a year-round calendar of events. Discover SA Get to know one of the world’s safest and most liveable cities. Discover Adelaide Discover Mount Gambier and the best of city-living in a tranquil country setting. Discover Mount Gambier Embrace the coastal lifestyle in sunny, seaside Whyalla. Discover Whyalla Explore our campuses Our campuses provide vibrant, collaborative environments where staff benefit from access to modern, purpose-built facilities and industry-standard tools and technologies that support teaching, research, and professional services. In addition to workspaces, our campuses offer a range of amenities that enhance staff wellbeing and engagement, including galleries, museums, cafés, eateries, and shared spaces open to the broader community. Discover our campuses Quick links Our culture and values Your transition to Adelaide University ‎ Study at Adelaide Life at Adelaide Research About How to apply Campuses Partners and community Support Frequently asked questions International students Alumni Staff directory Scholarships Accommodation News Contact us Tirkangkaku – Place of Learning Australian University Provider Number PRV14404 | CRICOS Provider Number 04249J Adelaide University respectfully acknowledges the Kaurna, Boandik and Barngarla First Nations Peoples and their Elders past and present, who are the First Nations’ Traditional Owners of the lands that are home to our campuses located in Adelaide, Magill, Mawson Lakes, Waite, Roseworthy, Mount Gambier and Whyalla. Disclaimer Copyright Privacy Accessibility This website uses cookies to ensure you get the best experience on our website, serve you personalised ads or content, and analyse our traffic. See our privacy notification for further details. To adjust your cookie settings, click 'More information'. By clicking 'Accept and continue', you consent to our use of cookies. Accept and continue More information Adjust your settings Essential cookies Preserves the visitor’s session state across multiple page requests. These cookies are required for basic site functionality and are therefore always enabled. Analytical cookies Used to send data through services such as Adobe Experience Platform and our advertising platform about a user's device and behaviour. Tracks the user across devices and marketing channels. Update my cookie preferences Cancel View Adelaide Uni’s Privacy Policy Back to top"
